## Motivation

The current academic peer review system is a critical component of scientific advancement and quality control, yet it is often time-consuming, biased, and arbitrary. This paper addresses these limitations by proposing a new system that incorporates large language models (LLMs) to assist in the peer review process, aiming to enhance its efficiency and quality. Existing methods, such as blind and open review systems, have their strengths and weaknesses, but they struggle to balance the subjectivity of research expertise, the effort required from reviewers, and the alignment between reviewers' opinions with overall ratings. The significance of this research lies in improving the review system to advance AI, data science, and information science, which have been identified as key areas for growth by the Artificial Intelligence Index. The paper's approach focuses on creating a review system that leverages LLMs to assist reviewers and provide community-wide guidance, thereby reducing bias and improving the overall quality of the peer review process.

## Main Idea

The paper introduces a pilot review system that incorporates large language models (LLMs) to assist in the peer review process of academic conferences. The proposed system, which includes Author-Assisted Evaluation (Author-AAE) and Community-Guided Review (CGR), aims to address the limitations of existing review methods by reducing subjectivity and enhancing the quality of reviews. The system allows authors to provide their own scores for their work, which are considered by LLM-based reviewers, and it also incorporates community feedback to refine the reviews. The results suggest that this approach improves the fairness and efficiency of the peer review process, with LLM-based reviews scoring highly in both 'Reproducibility and Quality' and 'Review Quality'.

