\subsection{Acceptance Results Analysis}
\label{sec:accept}
For these experiments, we utilize data collected from the \textsc{Aaai 2024} and \textsc{CoRLL 2024} conferences. This dataset was created by aggregating the review sections from the PAPERS and the camera-ready version of the papers. The overall acceptance rate for the PAPERS was 50.7\% for \textsc{Aaai 2024} and 59.3\% for \textsc{CoRLL 2024}. The \singlellm\ baseline received an acceptance rate of 3\% and 3.6\% for \textsc{Aaai 2024} and \textsc{CoRLL 2024}. The \textsc{ICML 2023} dataset was not included in this analysis because its overall acceptance rate of 22.5\% is very different from the above.
\paragraph{Methods}
We conduct a series of Z-tests for all combinations of datasets and reviewers. Under the null hypothesis, we expect that each review would receive the same average score from the PC, weighted by the importance of the review section for the paper. The results from this test are shown in Table~\ref{tab:acceptance-rates}. 

\paragraph{Results}
The results of the Z-test for each dataset are as follows. 
For \textsc{Aaai 2024}, AAE+CGR is similar to HUMAN (the null hypothesis cannot be rejected) and \singlellm\ (the null hypothesis also cannot be rejected). 

For the PAPERS of \textsc{CoRLL 2024}, AAE+CGR is similar to HUMAN and \singlellm\ (the null hypothesis cannot be rejected), and \reviewergpt\ is marginally significantly lower than all other methods (p-value of 0.495).

For the \textsc{ICML 2023} dataset, AAE+CGR and \singlellm\ are similar to HUMAN (the null hypothesis cannot be rejected).

In summary, we find that: (1) there is no evidence that the PC thought that the reviews from the AAE+CGR process were significantly lower than HUMAN or \singlellm; and (2) there is no evidence that the reviews of the AAE+CGR process were significantly higher than HUMAN or \singlellm. 
Overall, this provides evidence that our system will not negatively impact the outcomes that one would expect in an academic review process. 
A good reviewer will lead to outcomes that are expected by experts. 
