\subsection{Rebuttal Alignment Analysis}
We use the \textsc{ICML 2023} dataset to measure how well the reviews were aligned with the authors during the rebuttal phase~\citep{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1903-11367}. 

\paragraph{Method:}
We use the authors' scores to measure the quality of the papers. We then use a subset of the \textsc{ICML 2023} dataset, in which the authors \textbf{do not make a major revision}, resulting in the final decision to reject or accept the paper. The authors either did not provide a response to the PC request or refused to provide a score. 

The PC scores also represent the quality of the paper. We use a subset of the \textsc{ICML 2023} dataset where the PC \textbf{does not reject the paper}, so that we can ensure that their score is the final one, and that they would be accepted under the rules of the conference.
\paragraph{Results:}We use the Pearson correlation coefficient (R), Kendall's $\tau$ and Spearman's $\rho$ to measure the degree of correlation between owner scores and PC scores. Overall, there is a small but positive correlation between the owner and the PC scores, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:correlations} on the \textsc{ICML 2023} dataset.
